Files mbpm_export_perfmon.csv PerfMon CSV log format. Open it in Performance Monitor: View Log Data (Ctrl+L) > Source > Log files > Add (file type: CSV), then Data > Add and pick 'Much Better App Pool' / 'Much Better Machine'. It can be handled like any PerfMon CSV log (e.g. relog to .blg). mbpm_export_cpu.csv Plain CSV: utc_timestamp, local_timestamp, then CPU % of one core per pool.
Timestamps: mbpm_export_perfmon.csv uses local time, as PerfMon does (its header names the time zone and bias). The plain CSVs have both utc_timestamp (UTC, e.g. 2026-11-14T23:33:00.123Z) and local_timestamp (local time with its UTC offset, e.g. 2026-11-14T18:33:00.123-05:00): the same moment twice, so nothing needs converting and the hour repeated when daylight time ends is clear.
These counters are MBPM's own, computed from Windows counters; Windows has no 'Much Better App Pool' or 'Much Better Machine' counter set. Columns are per app pool, not per process: a replaced worker stays in its pool's column, and a pool's workers are summed.
Much Better App Pool(<pool>) CPU % of one core sum of \Process(w3wp*)\% Processor Time of the pool's workers (100 = one core) CPU % of machine CPU % of one core / logical processors WS MB \Process\Working Set, MB (Task Manager: Working set (memory)) Private WS MB \Process\Working Set - Private, MB (Task Manager: Memory (private working set)) Commit MB \Process\Private Bytes, MB (Task Manager: Commit size) GC MB \.NET CLR Memory\# Bytes in all Heaps, MB Native MB (approx) Private Bytes - \.NET CLR Memory\# Total committed Bytes, MB: native allocations plus the CLR's non-heap memory; an estimate, best read as a trend HTTP 500 % \W3SVC_W3WP(<PID>_<pool>)\% 500 HTTP Response Sent: status 500 only; for a web garden, the highest of the pool's workers .NET counters are matched to workers by their own Process ID counter; blank for workers without .NET. Workers are matched to pools by PID: \Process\ID Process with IIS's \W3SVC_W3WP(<PID>_<pool>) instances, or each w3wp command line's -ap argument (WMI) where those don't cover a worker yet.
Much Better Machine CPU % \Processor Information(_Total)\% Processor Time Memory used MB total physical memory - \Memory\Available MBytes Memory total MB total physical memory Commit MB \Memory\Committed Bytes, MB Commit limit MB \Memory\Commit Limit, MB Much Better CPU % MBPM's own CPU, % of all cores of the machine Sample delay ms how late each sample was taken against its schedule Missed samples samples skipped so far because MBPM fell a whole interval behind
Blank cells: no worker for that pool at that moment, or no data (e.g. a rate counter's first sample).
Written live, one row per sample, flushed every second; a pool that first appeared mid-session was added by rewriting the file once (its earlier cells are blank). To cut this export by time: relog mbpm_export_perfmon.csv -b <begin> -e <end> -f csv -o part.csv
